  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.i.s1",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.i.s1",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "Do not suppose, ye Greeks, that my separation from your customs is unreasonable and unthinking; for I found in them nothing that is holy or acceptable to God.",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.i.s2",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.i.s2",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "For the very compositions of your poets are monuments of madness and intemperance. For any one who becomes the scholar of your most eminent instructor, is more beset by difficulties than all men besides.",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.i.s3",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.i.s3",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "For first they say that Agamemnon, abetting the extravagant lust of his brother, and his madness and unrestrained desire,",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.i.s4",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.i.s4",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "readily gave even his daughter to be sacrificed, and troubled all Greece that he might rescue Helen, who had been ravished by the leprous shepherd.",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.i.s5",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.i.s5",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "But when in the course of the war they took captives, Agamemnon was himself taken captive by Chryseis, and for Briseis’ sake kindled a feud with the son of Thetis.",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.i.s6",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.i.s6",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "And Pelides himself, who crossed the river, overthrew Troy, and subdued Hector, this your hero became the slave of Polyxena, and was conquered by a dead Amazon;",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.i.s7",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.i.s7",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "and putting off the god-fabricated armour, and donning the hymeneal robe, he became a sacrifice of love in the temple of Apollo.",
    "chunk_index": 7,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.i.s8",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.i.s8",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "And the Ithacan Ulysses made a virtue of a vice.",
    "chunk_index": 8,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.i.s9",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.i.s9",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "And indeed his sailing past the Sirens gave evidence that he was destitute of worthy prudence, because he could not depend on his prudence for stopping his ears.",
    "chunk_index": 9,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.i.s10",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.i.s10",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "Ajax, son of Telamon, who bore the shield of sevenfold ox-hide, went mad when he was defeated in the contest with Ulysses for the armour.",
    "chunk_index": 10,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.i.s11",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.i.s11",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "Such things I have no desire to be instructed in. Of such virtue I am not covetous, that I should believe the myths of Homer.",
    "chunk_index": 11,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.i.s12",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.i.s12",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "For the whole rhapsody, the beginning and end both of the Iliad and the Odyssey is—a woman.",
    "chunk_index": 12,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.ii.s1",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.ii.s1",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "But since, next to Homer, Hesiod wrote his Works and Days, who will believe his drivelling theogony?",
    "chunk_index": 13,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.ii.s2",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.ii.s2",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "For they say that Chronos, the son of Ouranos, in the beginning slew his father, and possessed himself of his rule;",
    "chunk_index": 14,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.ii.s3",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.ii.s3",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "and that, being seized with a panic lest he should himself suffer in the same way, he preferred devouring his children;",
    "chunk_index": 15,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.ii.s4",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.ii.s4",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "but that, by the craft of the Curetes, Jupiter was conveyed away and kept in secret, and afterwards bound his father with chains, and divided the empire;",
    "chunk_index": 16,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.ii.s5",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.ii.s5",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "Jupiter receiving, as the story goes, the air, and Neptune the deep, and Pluto the portion of Hades.",
    "chunk_index": 17,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.ii.s6",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.ii.s6",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "But Pluto ravished Proserpine; and Ceres sought her child wandering through the deserts. And this myth was celebrated in the Eleusinian fire.",
    "chunk_index": 18,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.ii.s7",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.ii.s7",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "Again, Neptune ravished Melanippe when she was drawing water, besides abusing a host of Nereids not a few, whose names, were we to recount them, would cost us a multitude of words.",
    "chunk_index": 19,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.ii.s8",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.ii.s8",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "And as for Jupiter, he was a various adulterer, with Antiope as a satyr, with Danaë as gold, and with Europa as a bull;",
    "chunk_index": 20,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.ii.s9",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.ii.s9",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "with Leda, moreover, he assumed wings. For the love of Semele proved both his unchastity and the jealousy of Semele.",
    "chunk_index": 21,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.ii.s10",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.ii.s10",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "And they say that he carried off the Phrygian Ganymede to be his cup-bearer. These, then, are the exploits of the sons of Saturn.",
    "chunk_index": 22,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.ii.s11",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.ii.s11",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "And your illustrious son of Latona [Apollo], who professed soothsaying, convicted himself of lying.",
    "chunk_index": 23,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.ii.s12",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.ii.s12",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "He pursued Daphne, but did not gain possession of her; and to Hyacinthus, who loved him, he did not foretell his death.",
    "chunk_index": 24,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.ii.s13",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.ii.s13",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "And I say nothing of the masculine character of Minerva, nor of the feminine nature of Bacchus, nor of the fornicating disposition of Venus.",
    "chunk_index": 25,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.ii.s14",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.ii.s14",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "Read to Jupiter, ye Greeks, the law against parricides, and the penalty of adultery, and the ignominy of pæderasty.",
    "chunk_index": 26,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.ii.s15",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.ii.s15",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "Teach Minerva and Diana the works of women, and Bacchus the works of men.",
    "chunk_index": 27,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.ii.s16",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.ii.s16",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "What seemliness is there in a woman’s girding herself with armour, or in a man’s decorating himself with cymbals, and garlands, and female attire, and accompanied by a herd of bacchanalian women?",
    "chunk_index": 28,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.iii.s1",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.iii.s1",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "For Hercules, celebrated by his three nights, sung by the poets for his successful labours, the son of Jupiter, who slew the lion and destroyed the many-headed hydra;",
    "chunk_index": 29,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.iii.s2",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.iii.s2",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "who put to death the fierce and mighty boar, and was able to kill the fleet man-eating birds, and brought up from Hades the three-headed dog;",
    "chunk_index": 30,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
    "out_of_period": false,
    "translation_group_id": null
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks#PsJust.OrGr.iii.s3",
    "work_id": "anf-works/pseudo-justin-discourse-to-the-greeks",
    "canonical_ref": "PsJust.OrGr.iii.s3",
    "language": "eng",
    "text": "who effectually cleansed the huge Augean building from its dung, and killed the bulls and the stag whose nostrils breathed fire, and plucked the golden fruit from the tree, and slew the poisonous serpent (and for some reason,",
    "chunk_index": 31,
    "context_before": null,
    "context_after": null,
    "date_composed": 200,
    "date_range": [
      150,
      300
    ],
    "date_confidence": "uncertain",
